---
import HomeCode from '../components/HomeCode.astro';
import { url } from '../lib/urls';
import release from '../data/release.json';
const canonical=new URL(url(),Astro.site).href;
const ogImage=new URL(url('brand/rexafs-og.jpg'),Astro.site).href;
const stages=[
  ['Data','Import files, map columns and organize groups.','docs/desktop/import/'],
  ['Normalize','Find the edge and scale the absorption to a unit step.','docs/desktop/processing/#normalization'],
  ['Background','Remove the smooth background with AUTOBK to obtain χ(k).','docs/desktop/processing/#background'],
  ['Transform','Weight, window and Fourier-transform χ(k) into R space.','docs/desktop/processing/#forward-transform'],
  ['Fit','Calculate scattering paths and fit a structural model.','docs/desktop/fitting/'],
  ['Series','Browse a scan frame by frame and follow trends.','docs/desktop/series/'],
  ['Publish','Export figures, data tables, captions and a methods draft.','docs/desktop/publication/'],
];
const capabilities=[
  ['Import measured data','Read plain-text and XDI files. Choose transmission, fluorescence, reference or a ready μ column, confirm eV, keV or monochromator angle, and save the mapping as a reusable recipe.','docs/desktop/import/','Import guide'],
  ['Normalize and compare','Fit pre-edge and post-edge baselines, inspect the estimated edge energy, and overlay marked spectra. Align, calibrate, deglitch, rebin, smooth, merge or subtract spectra when needed.','docs/science/analysis/#data-treatment-choices','Data treatment'],
  ['Remove the background','AUTOBK estimates the smooth background with a cubic spline and a low-R criterion. rexafs adds a documented fixed endpoint penalty and solves it in one linear step.','docs/science/autobk/','AUTOBK objective'],
  ['Transform and filter','Apply a k weight and a Kaiser–Bessel, Hanning or other window, transform to R, and back-transform a selected R range to q. Every scale factor and unit is written down.','docs/science/processing/','Processing equations'],
  ['Build structural models','Start from a built-in structure, a CIF or XYZ file, or Materials Project, AMCSD and COD. Calculate scattering paths with ReFEFF or FEFF10 and inspect the cluster in 3D.','docs/desktop/structures/','Structures and paths'],
  ['Fit one or many spectra','Fit a single spectrum, share variables across several spectra, or fit a whole series as independent batches. Read uncertainties, correlations and the R-factor with their definitions.','docs/desktop/fitting/','First fit'],
  ['Analyze mixtures','Linear combination fitting with bounded weights and principal component analysis for collections of XANES or χ(k) spectra, with explicit preprocessing assumptions.','docs/science/analysis/','LCF and PCA'],
  ['Publish and reproduce','Save PNG or SVG figures at 300 DPI, export CSV curves, and write an analysis folder with a printable report, captions, processed arrays, a methods draft and references.','docs/desktop/publication/','Publication guide'],
];
const tour=[
  ['normalize','Normalize','The pre-edge and post-edge fit windows are drawn on the measured spectrum so you can judge the baseline before trusting the edge step.','docs/getting-started/first-analysis/#2-inspect-normalization'],
  ['background','Background','AUTOBK settings sit beside the weighted χ(k) they produce. Blank fields mean automatic, and placeholders show the resolved value.','docs/desktop/processing/#background'],
  ['paths','Structures and paths','Calculated scattering paths are grouped by shell next to the 3D cluster. First-shell and importance shortcuts give a starting model.','docs/desktop/structures/'],
  ['publication','Publish','Figure size, DPI, labels, limits and captions are set once, then exported as PNG, SVG, CSV or a complete analysis folder.','docs/desktop/publication/'],
];
const faq=[
  ['Is rexafs free?','Yes. rexafs is open source under the MIT or Apache-2.0 license, at your option. The desktop application, the libraries and this documentation are free to use, including for commercial work. Bundled calculation engines and dependencies keep their own notices.','licenses/','Licenses'],
  ['What is X-ray absorption spectroscopy?','XAS measures how strongly a sample absorbs X-rays as the photon energy crosses an element’s absorption edge. The near-edge region (XANES) reflects oxidation state and site symmetry. The extended oscillations (EXAFS) encode the distances, numbers and disorder of neighboring atoms. rexafs turns measured intensities into those quantities.','docs/concepts/','Concepts and glossary'],
  ['Do I need to program?','No. The desktop covers the complete workflow from import to publication without code. The Python, TypeScript and Rust libraries are for people who want the same processing inside scripts, notebooks, web tools or their own applications.','docs/getting-started/','Start here'],
  ['Which interfaces do what?','The desktop and the Rust crate offer the full workflow, including data treatment, LCF, PCA, structures and fitting. The Python and TypeScript packages currently expose spectrum processing: normalization, AUTOBK, forward and inverse transforms.','docs/getting-started/#features-by-interface','Feature map'],
  ['How does it relate to Athena, Artemis and Larch?','rexafs is an independent implementation with its own Rust engine, defaults and project format. Its processing is checked against XrayLarch as a regression reference, but numerical defaults such as the AUTOBK endpoint penalty differ. The Rust library can read Athena project files for interchange.','docs/science/fourier-compatibility/','Compatibility notes'],
  ['Does it work offline?','Yes. Processing, fitting, the bundled Cu example and the built-in structures work without a connection. Online structure databases, update checks and the optional assistant need connectivity.','docs/getting-started/updates/','Updates and offline use'],
  ['How should I cite it?','Cite the rexafs version and interface you used, the actual FEFF backend, and the primary papers behind the algorithms. The references page lists them and the analysis export writes a references file for you.','docs/science/references/','References and citations'],
  ['Where do I get help?','Read the troubleshooting page first, then open a GitHub issue with your version, operating system, exact error and a small example you are able to share.','docs/troubleshooting/','Troubleshooting'],
];
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head><meta charset="utf-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"/>
<title>rexafs — X-ray absorption analysis, your way</title>
<meta name="description" content="rexafs is an open-source desktop application and library set for X-ray absorption spectroscopy. Import measured spectra, normalize, remove the EXAFS background, Fourier transform, fit scattering-path models and export publication figures, with documented methods."/>
<link rel="canonical" href={canonical}/><link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href={url('favicon.svg')}/>
<meta property="og:title" content="rexafs — X-ray absorption analysis, your way"/><meta property="og:description" content="Open-source XAS and EXAFS analysis. One Rust engine for the desktop and for Python, TypeScript and Rust. Methods you can inspect."/><meta property="og:type" content="website"/><meta property="og:url" content={canonical}/><meta property="og:image" content={ogImage}/><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"/>
</head>
<body>
<a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a>
<header class="site-header wrap">
  <a class="brand" href={url()} aria-label="rexafs home"><img src={url('favicon.svg')} width="29" height="29" alt=""/>rexafs</a>
  <nav aria-label="Main navigation"><a href={url('docs/desktop/')}>Desktop</a><a href={url('docs/libraries/')}>Libraries</a><a href={url('docs/science/')}>Science</a><a href={url('download/')}>Download</a><a href={url('docs/getting-started/')}>Documentation <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></nav>
  <a class="github" href="https://github.com/Ameyanagi/rexafs">GitHub <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
</header>
<main id="main" class="wrap">
  <section class="intro">
    <a class="release-pill" href={url('releases/')}><span></span> STABLE {release.version} <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
    <p class="eyebrow">OPEN-SOURCE X-RAY ABSORPTION ANALYSIS</p>
    <h1>Your spectra.<br/><span>Your way to analyze.</span></h1>
    <p class="lede">From measured absorption to structural models.<br class="desktop-break"/> A shared Rust engine, built for the desktop and your code.</p>
    <p class="explainer">rexafs is a free, open-source toolkit for X-ray absorption spectroscopy (XAS). It reads the spectra you measure at a beamline, normalizes them, extracts the extended fine structure (EXAFS), transforms it to real space and fits scattering-path models to recover interatomic distances, coordination numbers and disorder. Every step is explained with its equations, units, defaults and references, so the result can be understood, reproduced and cited.</p>
    <ul class="facts" aria-label="Key facts">
      <li><strong>Free and open source</strong><span>MIT or Apache-2.0</span></li>
      <li><strong>Desktop, no coding</strong><span>macOS · Windows · Linux</span></li>
      <li><strong>Libraries</strong><span>Python · TypeScript · Rust</span></li>
      <li><strong>Portable projects</strong><span>Save, share and reopen analyses</span></li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section class="entry-grid" aria-label="Choose how to use rexafs">
    <article class="entry desktop-entry">
      <div class="entry-heading"><span class="number">01 /</span><h2><a href={url('docs/desktop/')}>Desktop</a></h2><span class="entry-label">EXPLORE VISUALLY</span></div>
      <p>Inspect spectra, refine a fit, and prepare figures.<br/> Keep the full analysis together in a portable project.</p>
      <a class="screenshot-link" href={url('docs/getting-started/first-analysis/')} aria-label="Start your first analysis with the rexafs desktop"><img src={url('screenshots/fit-result.jpg')} width="1192" height="768" alt="Complete rexafs 0.2.4 window showing the Cu first-shell fit, residuals and fitted parameters" loading="eager"/></a>
      <div class="entry-bottom"><a class="primary" href={url('download/')}>Download the desktop <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a class="secondary" href={url('docs/getting-started/first-analysis/')}>First analysis →</a></div>
      <div class="platforms">macOS <span>·</span> Windows preview <span>·</span> Linux preview <span>·</span> No Python or Rust installation required</div>
    </article>
    <article class="entry library-entry">
      <div class="entry-heading"><span class="number">02 /</span><h2><a href={url('docs/libraries/')}>Libraries</a></h2><span class="entry-label">BUILD WITH CODE</span></div>
      <p>Bring spectrum processing into your own workflow.<br/> Use NumPy, a browser, Node, or a native Rust application.</p>
      <HomeCode/>
      <div class="entry-bottom"><a class="primary" href={url('docs/libraries/')}>Use the libraries <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
      <nav class="api-links" aria-label="Library API references"><a href={url('docs/reference/stable/python/spectrum/')}>Python API →</a><a href={url('docs/reference/stable/typescript/spectrum/')}>TypeScript API →</a><a href={url('api/rust/rexafs/index.html')}>Rust API →</a></nav>
      <div class="platforms">Python 3.10–3.14 <span>·</span> Node 22+ and browsers via WebAssembly <span>·</span> Rust crate with full fitting API</div>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section class="capabilities" aria-labelledby="capabilities-title">
    <div class="section-intro"><p class="eyebrow">WHAT YOU CAN DO</p><h2 id="capabilities-title">Everything between the detector and the figure.</h2><p>rexafs covers the complete EXAFS workflow. Each capability links to the guide that explains its controls, its defaults and what the result does and does not establish.</p></div>
    <ul class="capability-grid">
      {capabilities.map(([title,text,href,label])=><li><h3>{title}</h3><p>{text}</p><a href={url(href)}>{label} →</a></li>)}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="workflow" aria-labelledby="workflow-title">
    <div class="section-intro"><p class="eyebrow">A CONNECTED WORKFLOW</p><h2 id="workflow-title">Follow the signal.</h2><p>Start with the data. Understand every transformation.</p></div>
    <ol>
      <li><span>01</span><a href={url('docs/desktop/import/')}><h3>Read & inspect</h3><p>Confirm columns, signals and energy units.</p></a></li>
      <li><span>02</span><a href={url('docs/science/processing/')}><h3>Process</h3><p>Normalize, remove background and transform.</p></a></li>
      <li><span>03</span><a href={url('docs/desktop/fitting/')}><h3>Model & fit</h3><p>Connect scattering paths to measured EXAFS.</p></a></li>
      <li><span>04</span><a href={url('docs/desktop/publication/')}><h3>Share & reproduce</h3><p>Export figures, data, settings and references.</p></a></li>
    </ol>
    <div class="stage-bar" aria-label="Desktop stages">
      <p class="stage-caption">The desktop follows the same order. Its stage bar is:</p>
      <ol class="stages">
        {stages.map(([name,text,href])=><li><a href={url(href)}><strong>{name}</strong><span>{text}</span></a></li>)}
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="tour" aria-labelledby="tour-title">
    <div class="section-intro"><p class="eyebrow">A LOOK INSIDE</p><h2 id="tour-title">See the desktop at work.</h2><p>Full, unedited application windows from rexafs {release.version} on macOS, processing the bundled Cu foil example. Select a figure to open the guide for that stage.</p></div>
    <div class="tour-grid">
      {tour.map(([file,title,text,href])=><figure><a href={url(href)}><img src={url(`screenshots/${file}.jpg`)} width="1192" height="768" loading="lazy" alt={`Complete rexafs ${release.version} window at the ${title} stage`}/></a><figcaption><strong>{title}</strong><span>{text}</span></figcaption></figure>)}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="interfaces" aria-labelledby="interfaces-title">
    <div class="section-intro"><p class="eyebrow">WHO IT IS FOR</p><h2 id="interfaces-title">Choose the interface that fits your work.</h2><p>All four share one numerical engine, so a spectrum processed in a script matches the desktop when the settings match.</p></div>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Interface</th><th scope="col">Best for</th><th scope="col">What it includes</th><th scope="col">Get started</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Desktop application</th><td>Beamline users and analysts who want to inspect, fit and publish without writing code</td><td>Import, groups, processing, data treatment, LCF and PCA, structures, ReFEFF and FEFF10 paths, single and joint fits, series, projects, publication exports</td><td><a href={url('download/')}>Download</a> · <a href={url('docs/getting-started/first-analysis/')}>First analysis</a></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Python</th><td>NumPy and Jupyter workflows, scripted batches, integration with existing analysis code</td><td>Spectrum processing with typed settings and NumPy results; a QAS transmission reader</td><td><a href={url('docs/libraries/python/')}>Python guide</a></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">TypeScript / JavaScript</th><td>Browser tools, Node services and web applications</td><td>The same spectrum processing through WebAssembly, with TypeScript declarations</td><td><a href={url('docs/libraries/typescript/')}>TypeScript guide</a></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Rust</th><td>Native applications and anyone who needs the complete API</td><td>Processing, parallel groups, data treatment, analysis, I/O, structures, fitting and optional plotting</td><td><a href={url('docs/libraries/rust/')}>Rust guide</a></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="science-band">
    <div><p class="eyebrow">METHODS YOU CAN INSPECT</p><h2>The explanation is part<br/>of the analysis.</h2></div>
    <div><p>Equations with defined units. Defaults with context. API documentation generated from the source. Learn what a result means, and where its assumptions end. Where rexafs departs from a published algorithm, the guide says so and links to the implementing code.</p><a href={url('docs/science/')}>Explore the science <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a href={url('docs/science/references/')}>References & citations <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a href={url('docs/concepts/')}>Concepts & glossary <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
  </section>

  <section class="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-title">
    <div class="section-intro"><p class="eyebrow">QUESTIONS</p><h2 id="faq-title">Before you start.</h2></div>
    <dl class="faq-list">
      {faq.map(([q,a,href,label])=><div><dt>{q}</dt><dd>{a} <a href={url(href)}>{label} →</a></dd></div>)}
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="closing"><div><p>One spectrum is enough to begin.</p><p class="closing-sub">The desktop ships with a measured Cu foil spectrum and a built-in Cu structure, so you can follow the complete workflow before touching your own data.</p></div><div class="closing-links"><a class="primary" href={url('download/')}>Get rexafs {release.version} <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a href={url('docs/getting-started/first-analysis/')}>Try the bundled Cu example →</a></div></section>
</main>
<footer class="wrap">
  <div class="footer-top">
    <div class="footer-brand"><a class="brand" href={url()}>rexafs</a><p>Rust-powered X-ray absorption analysis.<br/>Open source under MIT or Apache-2.0.</p></div>
    <nav aria-label="Footer" class="footer-nav">
      <div><h3>Use</h3><a href={url('download/')}>Download</a><a href={url('docs/getting-started/install/')}>Install</a><a href={url('docs/getting-started/first-analysis/')}>First analysis</a><a href={url('docs/libraries/')}>Libraries</a></div>
      <div><h3>Learn</h3><a href={url('docs/concepts/')}>Concepts & glossary</a><a href={url('docs/science/')}>Science</a><a href={url('docs/reference/')}>API reference</a><a href={url('docs/faq/')}>FAQ</a></div>
      <div><h3>Project</h3><a href={url('releases/')}>Releases</a><a href={url('licenses/')}>Licenses & data</a><a href="https://github.com/Ameyanagi/rexafs">Source on GitHub ↗</a><a href="https://github.com/Ameyanagi/rexafs/issues">Get help ↗</a></div>
    </nav>
  </div>
</footer>
</body>
</html>
